---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import RivalTable from '../../components/RivalTable.astro';
import UploadCta from '../../components/UploadCta.astro';
import { Analyzer } from '../../components/Analyzer';
import { colormatchApp } from '../../lib/rivals';

const cardPrice = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_CARD_PRICE ?? '$9.99';

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'Is Colorwise free?',
    answer:
      'Yes. The colour analysis on colorwise.me is free with no sign-up: you upload a selfie, pick your skin, hair and eye colours on it, and get one of twelve seasons with 18 colours and 8 to avoid. Their My Best Colors phone app sells a one-time premium unlock on top.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which is more accurate, Colorwise or colormatch?',
    answer:
      'Neither has published a test against professional draping, so any accuracy figure would be a guess. They fail in different ways: Colorwise depends on which pixels you pick as your skin, hair and eyes, and colormatch on how fair a record of your face the photo is. Running both on the same daylight photo and checking the overlap in a mirror is the honest test.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a good Colorwise.me alternative?',
    answer:
      'colormatch, if you would rather not pick your own skin tone on the photo: it measures the photo by itself, or asks seven questions if you have no photo you trust, and names every colour with its hex code. ColorMe, if you want a live mirror to judge palettes against your own face. Both are free and keep the photo on your device.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is there an AI color analysis tool that does not upload my photo?',
    answer:
      'Yes. colormatch reads the photo inside your browser and never sends it anywhere — it even works in aeroplane mode once the page has loaded. Colorwise and ColorMe also say the photo stays on your device. Tonebook sends it to OpenAI and color-analysis.app uploads it, by their own statements.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the best free seasonal color analysis app?',
    answer:
      'For a free twelve-season result, Colorwise. For a result you do not have to steer yourself, with every colour named and coded and a quiz when there is no good photo, colormatch. Both are free in the browser, with no account and no upload.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="colormatch vs Colorwise — Free Color Analysis Compared | colormatch"
  description="colormatch vs Colorwise.me: both free, both keep your photo in the browser. Colorwise gives 12 seasons if you pick your colours; colormatch reads the photo for you."
  path="/vs/colorwise/"
  crumb="colormatch vs Colorwise"
  schema={[colormatchApp(cardPrice)]}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }]}
  faq={faq}
>
  <section class="hero hero--split hero--rivals">
    <div class="shell hero__inner">
      <div class="hero__words stack">
        <h1>colormatch vs Colorwise</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Both are free, need no account and keep your photo in the browser. Colorwise gives one of twelve seasons once
          you pick your skin, hair and eye colours on the selfie. colormatch reads the photo by itself, or asks seven
          questions, and names and codes every colour.
        </p>
        <p class="hero__aside">
          Twelve seasons for free and happy to pick colours: Colorwise. Rather not judge your own skin tone: try
          colormatch here.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="hero__tool">
        <Analyzer client:load bareSamples />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint">
    <div class="shell stack-lg">
      <RivalTable
        ids={['colormatch', 'colorwise']}
        cardPrice={cardPrice}
        caption="colormatch and Colorwise side by side"
      />
      <UploadCta />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell grid-2 grid-2--top">
      <div class="stack">
        <h2>Where Colorwise is better</h2>
        <p>
          The free result goes further. You get a season out of twelve rather than four, and a palette card with
          eighteen colours and eight to avoid, without paying for anything.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is also more than one tool: a draping studio for trying fabrics against a photo, palettes from a picture of
          your wardrobe, a forum and a directory of stylists, and phone apps that check a colour through the camera
          while you shop.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <h2>Where colormatch is better</h2>
        <p>
          You do not have to decide what colour your own skin is. Colorwise asks you to click the pixels that represent
          your skin, hair and eyes, and the result follows from those clicks. colormatch finds the skin in the middle of
          the photo and measures undertone, value and contrast itself.
        </p>
        <p>
          It works without a photo: seven questions about your veins, jewellery, hair and eyes give the same kind of
          result. Every colour in your palette comes with a name and a hex code you can paste into a shop filter, and
          the pages are set in large type with high contrast, so they are easy to read at any age.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="narrow stack">
        <h2>How to use both</h2>
        <p>
          Take one photo by a window in daylight, with no filter and little make-up. Run it through both. If they agree on
          warm or cool and on light or deep, you have your answer. If they disagree, hold two colours from each palette
          under your chin in a mirror: the one that makes your skin look even and your eyes brighter wins.
        </p>
        <p>
          Comparing more tools? See the <a href="/alternatives/colorwise/">Colorwise alternatives</a> and the{' '}
          <a href="/alternatives/color-analysis-app/"><span class="nowrap">color-analysis.app</span> alternatives</a>, or{' '}
          <a href="/color-analysis-quiz/">take the seven-question quiz</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--close">
    <div class="shell stack-lg">
      <Faq title="colormatch and Colorwise: questions" items={faq} />
      <UploadCta />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
